WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:12.400
Hello everyone! Today, we will explain&nbsp;
how to get rid of too many dimensions.&nbsp;

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:17.840
Especially, after in the last video&nbsp;
we have learnt that they can be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:23.840
often a curse, so yes, in a sense,
today we learn how to lift curses.

00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:28.640
There are many methods for&nbsp;
dimensionality reduction,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:37.360
like PCA, ICA, BBA, Funny-yay,…
Ok, I messed up. Here, again:&nbsp;

00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:45.360
PCA, ICA, NMF, LDA, IDA, t-SNE,&nbsp;
UMAP, Autoencoders and so on.&nbsp;

00:00:46.640 --> 00:00:51.120
And all are awesome, coming with&nbsp;
their own solutions and tricks.

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:56.720
But today, we will discuss&nbsp;
the basics by example of PCA,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.720 --> 00:01:00.720
which stands for Principal Component Analysis.

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:07.200
In a nutshell: PCA uses some heuristics&nbsp;
to find the most important directions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:12.080
in the data, then it can discard&nbsp;
the most unimportant parts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.080 --> 00:01:16.320
in order to achieve the desired&nbsp;
dimensionality of the target space.

00:01:17.120 --> 00:01:23.360
If the data would be 2-dimensional, we&nbsp;
could even visualize it easily, like this.&nbsp;

00:01:24.160 --> 00:01:28.800
Looking at the data, clearly there are&nbsp;
more important dimensions than others.

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:35.600
In this direction, the spread is not that big and
if we would clamp all points onto one line,&nbsp;

00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:40.240
we would not lose that much information.
We would only lose a lot of information,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:43.840
if we would get rid of the&nbsp;
other direction of the spread,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:49.040
because there the spread is much bigger and&nbsp;
helps as a lot to differentiate the points.

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:55.680
So the main idea of the PCA algorithm is
to keep the directions of the biggest spread&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.680 --> 00:02:01.360
and throw away the rest until we reach&nbsp;
the desired number of dimensions, D prime.&nbsp;

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:09.280
But how to do that? With math, of course,&nbsp;
but first let’s think about this intuitively:

00:02:09.280 --> 00:02:12.720
What if we would approximate&nbsp;
our data with an ellipse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:18.080
basically a 2dimensional Gaussian and&nbsp;
find out the main axes of the ellipse?

00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:22.000
These axes, which are called&nbsp;
"principal components" are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:24.640
exactly the directions we are looking for.&nbsp;

00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:30.160
We can keep the most important principal&nbsp;
components and throw away the other ones!

00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:33.920
But intuition does not compute&nbsp;
stuff for us, math does.&nbsp;

00:02:34.480 --> 00:02:39.760
So let’s see how we could do this&nbsp;
mathematically, by setting up the PCA algorithm:&nbsp;

00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:45.520
First, let’s suppose we have our&nbsp;
data X which is a matrix with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.520 --> 00:02:51.840
rows containing all our data points. The columns&nbsp;
contain the features of these data points.

00:02:52.720 --> 00:02:58.560
Then, we want to centralize our data, meaning&nbsp;
that the mean of all points should be 0.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:02.160
How to do that?
By taking the data&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:04.320
and subtracting it’s mean. Done.

00:03:04.960 --> 00:03:08.000
After this simple transformation,&nbsp;
we are ready to go on.

00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:12.960
We want to find the biggest&nbsp;
spread or variance of the data,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.960 --> 00:03:18.960
because that is the informative part,&nbsp;
the part most unpredictable of the data.

00:03:18.960 --> 00:03:22.240
For that, we compute the&nbsp;
scatter matrix of the data,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.240 --> 00:03:27.440
defined by this formula. And remember,&nbsp;
we have already subtracted the mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:33.040
this is the reason why the formula for the&nbsp;
scatter matrix is not more complicated.

00:03:33.040 --> 00:03:36.000
But what is this thing, the scatter matrix?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.800 --> 00:03:43.600
We remember that our data X is composed&nbsp;
of rows of D-dimensional vectors.

00:03:43.600 --> 00:03:49.840
If we take one of these rows, transpose it and&nbsp;
do the multiplication, we get a DxD matrix.&nbsp;

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:54.960
And of course, we sum this&nbsp;
over all data samples i.

00:03:54.960 --> 00:03:56.560
Why have we done this? In order to be&nbsp;
able to apply our intuition, remember? We&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.560 --> 00:04:00.240
wanted to compute the main axes of the ellipse.

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:04.800
Well, it turns out that these&nbsp;
directions of the main axes are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:08.160
actually the eigenvectors of this scatter matrix.

00:04:08.800 --> 00:04:12.160
And each eigenvector, has a so-called eigenvalue,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.160 --> 00:04:17.680
which captures the importance of the&nbsp;
eigenvector, so the magnitude of the spread.

00:04:17.680 --> 00:04:22.800
So this is what we do, we take the scatter&nbsp;
matrix, compute eigenvalues and eigenvectors.

00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:31.200
For big matrices, we do it of course&nbsp;
in code, like with this in Python.

00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:37.200
Now that we have the eigenvectors, we can sort&nbsp;
them by eigenvalue to determine their importance.

00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:39.760
And we are ready to reduce the dimension!

00:04:40.400 --> 00:04:44.800
For this, we decide on how many&nbsp;
dimensions we want to reduce,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.800 --> 00:04:51.840
say D prime. So we take D’ eigenvectors and&nbsp;
put them into a matrix like this. (whisper: the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.840 --> 00:04:56.880
eigenvectors are D dimensional, since&nbsp;
they live in the original space)

00:04:56.880 --> 00:05:00.160
And what we want then, is&nbsp;
to compute the new position&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.160 --> 00:05:03.760
z in the new space of our data points, like this,&nbsp;

00:05:04.400 --> 00:05:11.840
meaning that we take out each data point x and&nbsp;
multiply it with this matrix V of eigenvectors.

00:05:11.840 --> 00:05:17.120
So we have x_i, a 1 timed&nbsp;
D matrix which is a vector,

00:05:17.120 --> 00:05:20.800
multiplied with a D times d prime matrix.&nbsp;

00:05:20.800 --> 00:05:25.520
What comes out is a 1 by D&nbsp;
prime matrix which is a vector,

00:05:25.520 --> 00:05:32.800
so vectors in our new, reduced D prime dimensional&nbsp;
space. So because V projects our points x from a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.800 --> 00:05:39.440
higher dimensional space to a lower dimensional&nbsp;
space D prime, we call V the projection matrix.

00:05:40.560 --> 00:05:46.320
What this projection matrix actually does&nbsp;
is it rotates the data, and maps it to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.320 --> 00:05:52.480
the new space with lower dimensionality&nbsp;
than the original one. And that was it!

00:05:52.480 --> 00:05:56.640
And the best part is, we only&nbsp;
have to understand the idea once,&nbsp;

00:05:56.640 --> 00:06:02.640
because the implementation of the algorithm is&nbsp;
already available in python with scikit-learn!

00:06:02.640 --> 00:06:04.640
Let’s have a short look at how to use it!

00:06:05.200 --> 00:06:10.640
This the one official scikit-learn examples,&nbsp;
where dimensionality reduction with PCA&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.640 --> 00:06:16.080
is applied to the "iris dataset" containing&nbsp;
different flowers with 4 different features&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.080 --> 00:06:20.560
to differentiate between them (for&nbsp;
example petal width and length).

00:06:21.280 --> 00:06:25.120
But we humans, can not really&nbsp;
visualize the 4 dimensions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.760 --> 00:06:29.360
until we do not reduce them&nbsp;
to at least 3 dimensions.

00:06:29.920 --> 00:06:32.400
And this is luckily, exactly what this code does.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.200 --> 00:06:38.000
While the code might look dense, we will&nbsp;
now have a gentle introduction into it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:43.280
because you really have to know,&nbsp;
that PCA relevant lines are very few.

00:06:43.280 --> 00:06:50.000
The first python lines are as always importing&nbsp;
specific packages like numpy for multidimensional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.000 --> 00:06:56.960
arrays, matplotlib pyplot for ploting and of&nbsp;
course sklearn for the Machine Learning part.

00:06:57.600 --> 00:07:01.600
Sklearn is so kind to provide&nbsp;
us also with datasets.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.600 --> 00:07:05.760
So we load the iris data and&nbsp;
save it into the variable “iris”.

00:07:06.560 --> 00:07:13.600
In Machine Learning, it is a custom to save the&nbsp;
data in a variable called X and the labels in y.&nbsp;

00:07:13.600 --> 00:07:18.160
We initialize the PCA model by&nbsp;
using the class from scikit learn.

00:07:18.160 --> 00:07:24.000
As a parameter, we specify the number&nbsp;
of principal components we want to keep,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:28.480
so the dimensionality of our target&nbsp;
space, D prime which is now 3.

00:07:29.040 --> 00:07:34.000
Basically, this line is where we&nbsp;
specify our intention to reduce&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.000 --> 00:07:39.680
from the original dimensionality of 4, to&nbsp;
3. And the cool part about scikit-learn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.680 --> 00:07:45.280
is that you can exchange this line with many&nbsp;
other dimensionality reduction algorithms!

00:07:45.280 --> 00:07:53.280
But now with PCA, we fit the model to the data&nbsp;
X, and we do not use or need any labels y, since&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.280 --> 00:07:59.280
they are not of any use in the PCA algorithm,&nbsp;
which is a blind signal separation technique.

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:05.200
With the fitted model to our data, which has&nbsp;
internally done all complicated things for us,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.200 --> 00:08:10.640
like: centralized the data, computed the&nbsp;
scatter matrix, computed eigenvectors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.640 --> 00:08:15.200
and sorted them after the eigenvalues,&nbsp;
formed the projection matrix with them

00:08:16.080 --> 00:08:21.040
so now it is ready to be applied on the&nbsp;
data to compute the new representation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.040 --> 00:08:23.200
which is stored here again in X.

00:08:23.760 --> 00:08:26.880
X is now three dimensional&nbsp;
and we are basically done.

00:08:27.520 --> 00:08:32.800
We can use the rest of the code to make the cool&nbsp;
3D visualization that you see here at the side.

00:08:33.440 --> 00:08:36.320
Is it really that easy? Yes, it is!

00:08:37.360 --> 00:08:41.840
Now you have no excuse not to use&nbsp;
dimensionality reduction when needed.

00:08:41.840 --> 00:08:43.680
Do not forget to like and subscribe!

00:08:44.560 --> 00:08:53.840
Okay, bye.

